course: dictionary
lesson: 10731
title: Upland: high ground away from the sea
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Upland means high land, hills or high ground away from the sea. Look at the words around it to check. «Rat and Mole were coming back after a long day's outing with Otter, hunting and exploring on the wide uplands» tells you the uplands are where the streams begin, so they must be high, away from the River.
explain: Upland can also be an adjective, a word that describes a noun. Upland farms are farms on high land, and upland birds live in the hills. Uplands with an s means more than one area of high ground.
explain: Think of the opposite to help you. The land below the uplands is the lowland. Ask yourself: is this place high and away from the sea? If it is, upland is a good match.
fact: Upland means high land, hills or high ground away from the sea.
fact: Uplands is the plural noun: several areas of high ground, such as the Scottish Highlands or the Pennines.
fact: Upland can also be an adjective, as in upland farms or upland birds.
fact: The land below the uplands is called the lowland.
fact: Upland weather is colder and wetter, and farms there often keep sheep more than crops.
exercise: «Rat and Mole were coming back after a long day's outing with Otter, hunting and exploring on the wide uplands.» What are the uplands here? | Quiet streets in a town | *High ground with hills, where the streams begin | The sea shore at low tide | A dark wood full of tall trees
exercise: What is the land below the uplands called? | *Lowland | Mountain | Riverbank | Upland
exercise: Farms on high land, with hills around them, are called ___ farms. Which word fits the gap? | Seaside | *Upland | Lowland | Harbour
test: Which meaning fits upland best? | Flat land next to the sea | A busy city street | *Hills and high ground away from the sea | A deep, dark valley
test: Which is true of upland weather? | Always sunny | Warmer than the sea in winter | Always hot and dry | *Colder and wetter
exam: Which sentence uses upland as an adjective? | The boys walked along the beach. | They swam in the river all afternoon. | *They bought upland farms with lots of sheep. | The train left the station at noon.
